---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Artifact from '../components/Artifact.astro';
---

<Base title="Reflection" description="Five things the semester changed my mind about, and the claims I still cannot make.">
  <p class="kicker">Reflection</p>
  <h1>Five things I changed my mind about</h1>
  <p class="lede">Including the one where I caused the hallucination myself.</p>

  <section class="r">
    <h2>A prompt is a request. The program is the enforcement.</h2>
    <p>
      I started the semester believing that a sufficiently careful prompt
      produces reliable output. It does not. A prompt shifts a distribution. It
      makes the good answer much more likely and the bad answer rare, and rare
      is not the same as impossible.
    </p>
    <p>
      The AiAgri validator is where this became concrete. The prompt says to
      return one of four actions. The validator intersects whatever came back
      with the list of four and drops the rest. The prompt asks for a confidence
      between zero and one. The validator clamps it, because a model will return
      1.5 and mean nothing by it. If the response will not parse at all, that
      becomes a warning flagged for a human, not an exception and not a silent
      skip.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every one of those checks assumes the prompt failed. That is the shift:
      from writing instructions I hope hold, to designing for the turn where
      they do not.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="r">
    <h2>Less context beat more context, in both projects, for different reasons.</h2>
    <p>
      I expected context management to be about fitting inside a window. It
      turned out to be about attention.
    </p>
    <p>
      On the farm side, batching four sensor nodes into a single prompt was
      cheaper and measurably worse. A small model asked to hold four sets of
      readings starts attributing one node's dryness to another. One call per
      node fixed it. On the chatbot side, loading a customer's full history
      dragged details from three weeks ago into a question about a bed frame; a
      ten-message window answered better.
    </p>
    <p>
      In both cases the winning move was to compute a summary rather than paste
      a log. The farm context is not an hour of readings, it is the latest
      value, the hour's minimum, average and maximum, the change over that hour,
      and how long since the node was last heard from. That is a smaller prompt
      and a better one, and the two facts are related.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="r">
    <h2>My first hallucination was my fault.</h2>
    <p>
      I wrote an example of the output I wanted. It mentioned rainfall and
      irrigation water. The database tracks neither. The model produced rainfall
      figures, because I had shown it rainfall figures and asked it to match.
    </p>
    <p>
      The fix was not a stronger instruction against making things up. That
      instruction does nothing, because the model is not aware it is making
      things up. The fix was naming the absent fields:
    </p>
    <Artifact label="the fix" tone="verify">Anything not provided (rainfall, water tank level,
forecast, valve state) is UNKNOWN. Never invent values.
If an unknown affects your decision, say so in "reason"
and lower confidence.</Artifact>
    <p>
      "Do not hallucinate" is advice. "Rainfall is unknown" is a fact the model
      can repeat back, and the clause about lowering confidence gives the
      uncertainty somewhere to go other than into a guess.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="r">
    <h2>Fluency and correctness are independent variables.</h2>
    <p>
      I knew this as a sentence before the semester. I did not believe it until
      a perfectly formatted APA reference turned out to be wrong in author
      order, year and page range at the same time, and nothing in the text gave
      it away.
    </p>
    <p>
      What I take from that is a sorting rule rather than a general caution.
      Reasoning I can evaluate by following it. Arbitrary facts — a page range,
      a version number, a date — I cannot, because there is no chain to check.
      Those are exactly the places a model is operating on recall rather than
      inference, and exactly the places it is most confident and least grounded.
      So: <strong>verify the arbitrary, read the reasoned.</strong>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="r">
    <h2>The best conversational design decision I made was about when to stop talking.</h2>
    <p>
      The Betis bot sets a <code>bot_paused</code> flag on a complaint and alerts
      staff. The farm agent recommends and never actuates. The glasses stay
      silent below 0.8 confidence rather than guessing at a direction.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three different systems, the same shape. In each one the valuable design
      work was identifying the moment where continuing to be helpful becomes
      the harm, and building an exit. An angry customer is precisely the case
      where a fluent, friendly, slightly wrong answer does the most damage, and
      the correct response is to stop being a chatbot.
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2 class="limits-h">Claims I cannot make</h2>
  <p>
    The most useful thing in my security report was the table of what I did not
    fix. Same discipline here.
  </p>
  <ul class="limits">
    <li>
      <strong>Nothing has run on a real farm.</strong> The AiAgri base station
      works on simulated sensor data. The LoRa hardware is still in development,
      and mesh networking and solar power are the untested parts.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The chatbot has not met the public.</strong> It works in Meta's
      development mode with app testers. App Review is pending, and the
      thirty-to-fifty-question evaluation set is designed but not yet scored at
      full size.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>I have no bias evaluation worth the name.</strong> The course
      outcome names bias as a metric. I tested language handling for Tagalog,
      English and Taglish, which is coverage, not a bias measurement. I would
      not know how to design a real one for a furniture bot, and I would rather
      say that than dress up the language testing as something it is not.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>My evaluation is qualitative.</strong> Correct, wrong, or
      should-have-handed-off, scored by me. That catches obvious failure and
      misses systematic drift, and it does not survive the fact that I wrote
      both the prompts and the rubric.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where I want to go next</h2>
  <p>
    The gap I feel most is automated evaluation. Everything I checked this
    semester, I checked by hand, and the honest limit of that is my own
    attention on the day. A regression suite that replays a fixed question set
    against the bot after every knowledge base change would have caught things
    my reading did not, and it is the obvious next build.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second gap is adversarial testing done properly. I tried to break my own
    bot with discount requests and off-topic questions, which is a start, but I
    was testing as its author. Someone who wants a free quote out of a furniture
    shop will try things I did not think of.
  </p>
</Base>

